1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,401
Sweetheart, when anybody in our family was

2
00:00:03,401 --> 00:00:04,454
sick, or sad,

3
00:00:04,454 --> 00:00:05,912
or had a new baby,

4
00:00:05,912 --> 00:00:07,288
or lost somebody,

5
00:00:07,288 --> 00:00:10,123
Grandma Pearl showed up at the door

6
00:00:10,123 --> 00:00:12,715
with a big enamel pot wrapped in

7
00:00:12,715 --> 00:00:13,363
a towel.

8
00:00:13,543 --> 00:00:15,563
Chicken and dumplings.

9
00:00:15,743 --> 00:00:17,224
She never called first,

10
00:00:17,224 --> 00:00:19,284
she never asked what you needed.

11
00:00:19,464 --> 00:00:20,845
She just knew.

12
00:00:21,025 --> 00:00:23,075
And I've done the same thing for

13
00:00:23,075 --> 00:00:25,572
sixty years, because there is nothing a

14
00:00:25,572 --> 00:00:28,070
pot of chicken and dumplings can't make

15
00:00:28,070 --> 00:00:29,094
a little better.

16
00:00:29,444 --> 00:00:32,761
But today, when young people make dumplings,

17
00:00:32,761 --> 00:00:35,023
they come out heavy and gummy,

18
00:00:35,023 --> 00:00:36,154
like wet paste,

19
00:00:36,154 --> 00:00:38,491
or they fall apart in the broth

20
00:00:38,491 --> 00:00:40,602
until it's just cloudy soup.

21
00:00:40,782 --> 00:00:44,054
So tonight I'm giving you Grandma Pearl's

22
00:00:44,054 --> 00:00:44,613
recipe.

23
00:00:44,793 --> 00:00:47,615
Light, fluffy dumplings on top of a

24
00:00:47,615 --> 00:00:49,147
rich chicken broth.

25
00:00:49,327 --> 00:00:52,108
Every step, and the four mistakes that

26
00:00:52,108 --> 00:00:53,646
make dumplings gummy.

27
00:00:53,826 --> 00:00:54,984
It feeds six,

28
00:00:54,984 --> 00:00:57,567
and it costs less than a fast

29
00:00:57,567 --> 00:00:59,259
food lunch for two.

30
00:00:59,439 --> 00:01:00,645
And stay with me,

31
00:01:00,645 --> 00:01:03,271
because the fourth mistake is the one

32
00:01:03,271 --> 00:01:04,903
nearly everybody makes,

33
00:01:04,903 --> 00:01:06,961
and it has nothing to do with

34
00:01:06,961 --> 00:01:07,671
the dough.

35
00:01:08,020 --> 00:01:10,299
Grandma Pearl grew up on a farm

36
00:01:10,299 --> 00:01:13,680
outside Mansfield, and during the Depression a

37
00:01:13,680 --> 00:01:16,031
chicken had to feed nine people.

38
00:01:16,211 --> 00:01:18,104
So nothing was wasted.

39
00:01:18,284 --> 00:01:20,380
The broth came from the bones,

40
00:01:20,380 --> 00:01:22,477
the fat went in the dumplings,

41
00:01:22,477 --> 00:01:24,923
and the leftover broth was soup the

42
00:01:24,923 --> 00:01:25,552
next day.

43
00:01:25,732 --> 00:01:26,840
She used to say,

44
00:01:26,840 --> 00:01:28,849
a chicken in the pot is worth

45
00:01:28,849 --> 00:01:30,027
two on the table.

46
00:01:30,378 --> 00:01:33,242
She made her dumplings without measuring a

47
00:01:33,242 --> 00:01:34,743
single thing, by feel.

48
00:01:34,923 --> 00:01:37,152
It took me thirty years to write

49
00:01:37,152 --> 00:01:38,336
it down properly.

50
00:01:38,516 --> 00:01:40,712
I stood next to her in nineteen

51
00:01:40,712 --> 00:01:43,757
sixty-eight with a notebook and a measuring

52
00:01:43,757 --> 00:01:46,095
cup, and every time she grabbed a

53
00:01:46,095 --> 00:01:47,299
handful of flour,

54
00:01:47,299 --> 00:01:49,070
I made her drop it in the

55
00:01:49,070 --> 00:01:49,778
cup first.

56
00:01:49,958 --> 00:01:52,793
She thought I was the silliest girl

57
00:01:52,793 --> 00:01:53,441
in Ohio.

58
00:01:53,621 --> 00:01:55,511
But that's why I can give it

59
00:01:55,511 --> 00:01:56,523
to you tonight.

60
00:01:56,874 --> 00:01:59,290
The first time I made them myself

61
00:01:59,290 --> 00:02:01,999
was the winter of nineteen sixty-six,

62
00:02:01,999 --> 00:02:03,097
for my husband,

63
00:02:03,097 --> 00:02:04,269
who had the flu.

64
00:02:04,449 --> 00:02:06,609
I lifted the lid three times to

65
00:02:06,609 --> 00:02:09,116
check, and they came out like hockey

66
00:02:09,116 --> 00:02:09,534
pucks.

67
00:02:09,714 --> 00:02:11,827
He ate every one and said they

68
00:02:11,827 --> 00:02:14,433
were wonderful, because he was a good

69
00:02:14,433 --> 00:02:16,053
man and he had a fever.

70
00:02:16,233 --> 00:02:18,291
The next week I went straight to

71
00:02:18,291 --> 00:02:20,413
Pearl's kitchen with my notebook.

72
00:02:20,762 --> 00:02:21,970
For the chicken,

73
00:02:21,970 --> 00:02:22,423
honey.

74
00:02:22,603 --> 00:02:25,717
About three pounds of chicken pieces with

75
00:02:25,717 --> 00:02:27,084
the bone and skin.

76
00:02:27,264 --> 00:02:29,208
Thighs and legs are best,

77
00:02:29,208 --> 00:02:31,853
they have the most flavor and they

78
00:02:31,853 --> 00:02:32,941
don't dry out.

79
00:02:33,121 --> 00:02:35,237
One onion, two carrots,

80
00:02:35,237 --> 00:02:37,168
two stalks of celery,

81
00:02:37,168 --> 00:02:38,180
a bay leaf,

82
00:02:38,180 --> 00:02:41,399
a teaspoon of salt and some pepper.

83
00:02:41,579 --> 00:02:43,553
And about ten cups of water,

84
00:02:43,553 --> 00:02:45,457
enough to cover everything.

85
00:02:45,807 --> 00:02:47,177
For the dumplings.

86
00:02:47,357 --> 00:02:48,913
Two cups of flour.

87
00:02:49,093 --> 00:02:51,787
One tablespoon of baking powder.

88
00:02:51,967 --> 00:02:54,126
One teaspoon of salt.

89
00:02:54,306 --> 00:02:56,790
Three tablespoons of cold butter,

90
00:02:56,790 --> 00:02:59,424
or chicken fat skimmed from the pot

91
00:02:59,424 --> 00:03:00,552
like Pearl did.

92
00:03:00,732 --> 00:03:03,156
And about three quarters of a cup

93
00:03:03,156 --> 00:03:04,111
of cold milk.

94
00:03:04,291 --> 00:03:05,440
That's all.

95
00:03:05,620 --> 00:03:07,536
Not one thing fancy.

96
00:03:07,716 --> 00:03:09,681
In a minute I'll tell you the

97
00:03:09,681 --> 00:03:12,325
step that decides whether they come out

98
00:03:12,325 --> 00:03:13,680
light or like paste,

99
00:03:13,680 --> 00:03:15,645
and it takes no skill at all.

100
00:03:15,995 --> 00:03:18,185
You can use a whole chicken cut

101
00:03:18,185 --> 00:03:20,727
up, or a store rotisserie chicken if

102
00:03:20,727 --> 00:03:21,999
you're in a hurry,

103
00:03:21,999 --> 00:03:22,776
sweetheart.

104
00:03:22,956 --> 00:03:25,693
Pearl would never forgive me for saying

105
00:03:25,693 --> 00:03:26,044
that.

106
00:03:26,224 --> 00:03:27,810
But on a busy Tuesday,

107
00:03:27,810 --> 00:03:30,621
pull the meat off a rotisserie chicken,

108
00:03:30,621 --> 00:03:33,289
simmer the bones for forty minutes in

109
00:03:33,289 --> 00:03:35,379
a box of store broth with the

110
00:03:35,379 --> 00:03:38,263
vegetables, and you'll have supper in an

111
00:03:38,263 --> 00:03:38,623
hour.

112
00:03:38,803 --> 00:03:40,499
Not as good as hers,

113
00:03:40,499 --> 00:03:43,127
but close enough to make a sick

114
00:03:43,127 --> 00:03:44,144
child smile.

115
00:03:44,493 --> 00:03:45,457
Step one.

116
00:03:45,637 --> 00:03:48,373
Put the chicken in your biggest pot

117
00:03:48,373 --> 00:03:50,796
with the onion cut in quarters,

118
00:03:50,796 --> 00:03:53,298
one carrot, one stalk of celery,

119
00:03:53,298 --> 00:03:54,314
the bay leaf,

120
00:03:54,314 --> 00:03:55,877
the salt and pepper.

121
00:03:56,057 --> 00:03:58,217
Cover it with the cold water.

122
00:03:58,397 --> 00:03:59,736
Bring it to a boil,

123
00:03:59,736 --> 00:04:01,782
then turn it down to a gentle

124
00:04:01,782 --> 00:04:03,404
simmer, little bubbles,

125
00:04:03,404 --> 00:04:05,519
and leave it alone for an hour

126
00:04:05,519 --> 00:04:06,930
with the lid tilted.

127
00:04:07,110 --> 00:04:09,107
Skim off the gray foam that comes

128
00:04:09,107 --> 00:04:10,802
up in the first ten minutes.

129
00:04:11,151 --> 00:04:12,208
Step two.

130
00:04:12,388 --> 00:04:14,532
Take the chicken out and let it

131
00:04:14,532 --> 00:04:15,639
cool on a plate.

132
00:04:15,819 --> 00:04:18,194
Pour the broth through a strainer and

133
00:04:18,194 --> 00:04:20,184
throw out the tired vegetables.

134
00:04:20,364 --> 00:04:22,280
They gave everything they had.

135
00:04:22,460 --> 00:04:24,809
When the chicken is cool enough,

136
00:04:24,809 --> 00:04:27,012
pull the meat off the bones in

137
00:04:27,012 --> 00:04:29,435
big bites and throw away the skin

138
00:04:29,435 --> 00:04:30,169
and bones.

139
00:04:30,349 --> 00:04:32,839
Now chop the other carrot and celery

140
00:04:32,839 --> 00:04:34,984
fresh, and put them back in the

141
00:04:34,984 --> 00:04:37,059
broth to cook for ten minutes.

142
00:04:37,239 --> 00:04:38,940
If this is helping you,

143
00:04:38,940 --> 00:04:41,454
honey, tap the like button for me.

144
00:04:41,634 --> 00:04:44,374
It helps Grandma more than you know.

145
00:04:44,723 --> 00:04:47,364
And here's Pearl's trick for a broth

146
00:04:47,364 --> 00:04:49,124
that tastes like Sunday.

147
00:04:49,304 --> 00:04:51,909
Before you add anything back,

148
00:04:51,909 --> 00:04:52,717
taste it.

149
00:04:52,897 --> 00:04:55,567
Most people under-salt their broth,

150
00:04:55,567 --> 00:04:58,772
and then the dumplings taste like nothing.

151
00:04:58,952 --> 00:05:00,824
Add salt a little at a time

152
00:05:00,824 --> 00:05:03,320
until it tastes good enough to drink

153
00:05:03,320 --> 00:05:04,083
from a cup.

154
00:05:04,263 --> 00:05:06,237
That's how you know it's right.

155
00:05:06,417 --> 00:05:08,987
She always made me drink a little

156
00:05:08,987 --> 00:05:09,765
cup of it,

157
00:05:09,765 --> 00:05:10,933
and I still do.

158
00:05:11,283 --> 00:05:13,333
Then make it a little thicker,

159
00:05:13,333 --> 00:05:15,040
so it's a gravy and not a

160
00:05:15,040 --> 00:05:15,382
soup.

161
00:05:15,562 --> 00:05:18,617
Shake two tablespoons of flour with half

162
00:05:18,617 --> 00:05:20,450
a cup of cold water in a

163
00:05:20,450 --> 00:05:22,894
jar, the same trick as my gravy,

164
00:05:22,894 --> 00:05:25,721
and stir it into the simmering broth.

165
00:05:25,901 --> 00:05:27,762
Add the chicken back in,

166
00:05:27,762 --> 00:05:30,011
and a splash of milk or cream

167
00:05:30,011 --> 00:05:31,717
if you like it richer.

168
00:05:31,897 --> 00:05:34,112
Keep it at a gentle bubble,

169
00:05:34,112 --> 00:05:35,589
never a hard boil.

170
00:05:35,940 --> 00:05:38,389
Step three, the dumplings.

171
00:05:38,569 --> 00:05:39,683
Stir the flour,

172
00:05:39,683 --> 00:05:42,133
baking powder and salt in a bowl.

173
00:05:42,313 --> 00:05:44,698
Cut the cold butter in with two

174
00:05:44,698 --> 00:05:46,699
knives or your fingertips,

175
00:05:46,699 --> 00:05:49,314
until it looks like coarse crumbs.

176
00:05:49,494 --> 00:05:51,801
Then pour in the cold milk and

177
00:05:51,801 --> 00:05:53,107
stir with a fork,

178
00:05:53,107 --> 00:05:55,798
just until it comes together into a

179
00:05:55,798 --> 00:05:57,412
shaggy, sticky dough.

180
00:05:57,592 --> 00:05:59,775
Ten or twelve stirs.

181
00:05:59,955 --> 00:06:00,779
Stop.

182
00:06:00,959 --> 00:06:02,550
Lumps are fine.

183
00:06:02,730 --> 00:06:04,169
Lumps are good.

184
00:06:04,520 --> 00:06:05,390
Step four.

185
00:06:05,570 --> 00:06:07,984
Drop the dough by big spoonfuls on

186
00:06:07,984 --> 00:06:09,759
top of the bubbling stew,

187
00:06:09,759 --> 00:06:11,890
about the size of a golf ball,

188
00:06:11,890 --> 00:06:14,162
close together but not touching.

189
00:06:14,342 --> 00:06:16,408
They'll double in size.

190
00:06:16,588 --> 00:06:17,933
Use two spoons,

191
00:06:17,933 --> 00:06:20,442
one to scoop and one to push

192
00:06:20,442 --> 00:06:21,070
it off.

193
00:06:21,250 --> 00:06:23,624
Dip the spoons in the hot broth

194
00:06:23,624 --> 00:06:26,381
first so the dough slides right off.

195
00:06:26,561 --> 00:06:28,716
Now, the next step is the one

196
00:06:28,716 --> 00:06:30,648
that makes or breaks them.

197
00:06:30,998 --> 00:06:33,679
Step five, the most important one.

198
00:06:33,859 --> 00:06:36,011
Put the lid on tight and cook

199
00:06:36,011 --> 00:06:37,496
for fifteen minutes,

200
00:06:37,496 --> 00:06:39,351
and do not lift that lid.

201
00:06:39,531 --> 00:06:40,680
Not once.

202
00:06:40,860 --> 00:06:42,161
Not to peek.

203
00:06:42,341 --> 00:06:44,559
The dumplings cook in the steam,

204
00:06:44,559 --> 00:06:46,777
and every time you lift the lid,

205
00:06:46,777 --> 00:06:49,272
the steam escapes and they go heavy.

206
00:06:49,452 --> 00:06:52,195
Grandma Pearl would slap your hand away

207
00:06:52,195 --> 00:06:53,109
from the pot.

208
00:06:53,289 --> 00:06:56,691
She said, a watched dumpling never rises.

209
00:06:57,040 --> 00:06:58,752
After fifteen minutes,

210
00:06:58,752 --> 00:07:01,396
lift the lid and poke one dumpling

211
00:07:01,396 --> 00:07:02,718
with a toothpick,

212
00:07:02,718 --> 00:07:04,274
right in the middle.

213
00:07:04,454 --> 00:07:06,147
If it comes out clean,

214
00:07:06,147 --> 00:07:07,147
they're done.

215
00:07:07,327 --> 00:07:08,266
If it's wet,

216
00:07:08,266 --> 00:07:10,536
put the lid back on for three

217
00:07:10,536 --> 00:07:11,553
more minutes.

218
00:07:11,733 --> 00:07:14,532
They should be fluffy and light inside,

219
00:07:14,532 --> 00:07:15,609
like a biscuit,

220
00:07:15,609 --> 00:07:17,762
and a little bit glossy on top

221
00:07:17,762 --> 00:07:18,838
from the steam.

222
00:07:19,188 --> 00:07:22,400
Some people like rolled dumplings instead,

223
00:07:22,400 --> 00:07:23,776
flat like noodles,

224
00:07:23,776 --> 00:07:26,375
the way they make them down South.

225
00:07:26,555 --> 00:07:29,076
Same dough with a little less baking

226
00:07:29,076 --> 00:07:31,877
powder, rolled thin on a floured counter

227
00:07:31,877 --> 00:07:33,277
and cut into strips.

228
00:07:33,457 --> 00:07:35,434
Drop them in one at a time

229
00:07:35,434 --> 00:07:36,650
and stir gently.

230
00:07:36,830 --> 00:07:38,822
Pearl made the fluffy ones,

231
00:07:38,822 --> 00:07:40,371
but our neighbor Mrs.

232
00:07:40,551 --> 00:07:42,185
Hollis was from Kentucky,

233
00:07:42,185 --> 00:07:43,950
and she made the flat ones,

234
00:07:43,950 --> 00:07:46,238
and those two women argued about it

235
00:07:46,238 --> 00:07:48,330
over the fence for twenty years.

236
00:07:48,680 --> 00:07:50,424
So the four mistakes,

237
00:07:50,424 --> 00:07:51,338
sweetheart.

238
00:07:51,518 --> 00:07:52,853
Mistake one.

239
00:07:53,033 --> 00:07:55,193
Stirring the dough too much.

240
00:07:55,373 --> 00:07:57,121
If you beat it smooth,

241
00:07:57,121 --> 00:07:58,234
you get tough,

242
00:07:58,234 --> 00:07:59,506
gummy dumplings.

243
00:07:59,686 --> 00:08:01,567
A few stirs and stop.

244
00:08:01,747 --> 00:08:03,244
Mistake two.

245
00:08:03,424 --> 00:08:05,665
Warm butter and warm milk.

246
00:08:05,845 --> 00:08:08,745
Cold makes the little pockets that puff

247
00:08:08,745 --> 00:08:08,968
up.

248
00:08:09,148 --> 00:08:11,447
Keep them in the icebox until the

249
00:08:11,447 --> 00:08:12,840
second you use them.

250
00:08:13,020 --> 00:08:14,362
And mistake four,

251
00:08:14,362 --> 00:08:16,731
I'll say it again in a minute,

252
00:08:16,731 --> 00:08:19,731
because nobody listens the first time.

253
00:08:20,081 --> 00:08:21,706
Mistake three.

254
00:08:21,886 --> 00:08:24,057
A broth that isn't bubbling.

255
00:08:24,237 --> 00:08:26,441
If you drop the dough into broth

256
00:08:26,441 --> 00:08:27,612
that's just warm,

257
00:08:27,612 --> 00:08:29,886
the dumplings soak it up and turn

258
00:08:29,886 --> 00:08:30,437
to mush.

259
00:08:30,617 --> 00:08:32,549
It has to be at a steady

260
00:08:32,549 --> 00:08:33,113
simmer.

261
00:08:33,293 --> 00:08:34,680
And mistake four,

262
00:08:34,680 --> 00:08:35,986
lifting the lid.

263
00:08:36,166 --> 00:08:38,140
I know I already said it.

264
00:08:38,320 --> 00:08:41,506
I'm saying it again because everybody does

265
00:08:41,506 --> 00:08:41,733
it.

266
00:08:42,083 --> 00:08:44,795
And one more thing about the pot,

267
00:08:44,795 --> 00:08:45,288
honey.

268
00:08:45,468 --> 00:08:46,894
Use a wide pot,

269
00:08:46,894 --> 00:08:48,986
not a tall narrow one.

270
00:08:49,166 --> 00:08:51,934
The dumplings need room on top to

271
00:08:51,934 --> 00:08:53,276
spread and rise.

272
00:08:53,456 --> 00:08:54,746
If they're crowded,

273
00:08:54,746 --> 00:08:56,851
the ones in the middle stay raw

274
00:08:56,851 --> 00:08:58,888
while the ones on the edge get

275
00:08:58,888 --> 00:08:59,295
mushy.

276
00:08:59,475 --> 00:09:02,006
Pearl's enamel pot was so wide it

277
00:09:02,006 --> 00:09:03,540
covered two burners,

278
00:09:03,540 --> 00:09:05,687
and it's in my pantry today.

279
00:09:06,037 --> 00:09:07,690
Serve it in deep bowls,

280
00:09:07,690 --> 00:09:09,774
a few dumplings on top of the

281
00:09:09,774 --> 00:09:11,068
chicken and broth,

282
00:09:11,068 --> 00:09:13,799
with black pepper and a little chopped

283
00:09:13,799 --> 00:09:15,452
parsley if you have it.

284
00:09:15,632 --> 00:09:17,645
In our house we had it with

285
00:09:17,645 --> 00:09:20,403
a plate of sliced tomatoes in summer,

286
00:09:20,403 --> 00:09:22,192
or applesauce in winter.

287
00:09:22,372 --> 00:09:24,488
And always on a cold night,

288
00:09:24,488 --> 00:09:27,231
with the windows fogged up from the

289
00:09:27,231 --> 00:09:27,701
steam.

290
00:09:28,051 --> 00:09:31,181
Leftovers keep three days in the icebox,

291
00:09:31,181 --> 00:09:33,920
but the dumplings soak up the broth

292
00:09:33,920 --> 00:09:34,703
overnight.

293
00:09:34,883 --> 00:09:37,222
So warm it slowly with a little

294
00:09:37,222 --> 00:09:38,807
extra water or broth,

295
00:09:38,807 --> 00:09:40,015
and stir gently.

296
00:09:40,195 --> 00:09:42,880
My father liked it better the second

297
00:09:42,880 --> 00:09:43,178
day.

298
00:09:43,358 --> 00:09:45,506
He said the dumplings had time to

299
00:09:45,506 --> 00:09:46,481
think about it.

300
00:09:46,832 --> 00:09:48,714
And if you want to do what

301
00:09:48,714 --> 00:09:51,031
Pearl did, make a double pot and

302
00:09:51,031 --> 00:09:53,565
take half to somebody who needs it.

303
00:09:53,745 --> 00:09:55,374
Wrap the pot in a towel,

304
00:09:55,374 --> 00:09:56,799
put it in a box so it

305
00:09:56,799 --> 00:09:58,360
doesn't tip in the car,

306
00:09:58,360 --> 00:10:00,260
and leave it on the porch if

307
00:10:00,260 --> 00:10:02,093
they're not up for company.

308
00:10:02,273 --> 00:10:03,863
Don't call first.

309
00:10:04,043 --> 00:10:06,133
Just knock and go.

310
00:10:06,484 --> 00:10:08,071
So one more time,

311
00:10:08,071 --> 00:10:08,631
honey.

312
00:10:08,811 --> 00:10:11,203
Bone-in chicken simmered an hour.

313
00:10:11,383 --> 00:10:12,570
Strain the broth,

314
00:10:12,570 --> 00:10:14,873
salt it until it's good enough to

315
00:10:14,873 --> 00:10:17,037
drink, and thicken it a little.

316
00:10:17,217 --> 00:10:19,712
Cold butter, cold milk,

317
00:10:19,712 --> 00:10:21,014
a few stirs.

318
00:10:21,194 --> 00:10:23,587
Drop them on a bubbling pot,

319
00:10:23,587 --> 00:10:24,698
lid on tight,

320
00:10:24,698 --> 00:10:27,091
fifteen minutes, no peeking.

321
00:10:27,271 --> 00:10:30,503
That's Grandma Pearl's chicken and dumplings,

322
00:10:30,503 --> 00:10:33,088
and they have never once been gummy.

323
00:10:33,438 --> 00:10:35,396
Tell me in the comments,

324
00:10:35,396 --> 00:10:36,294
sweetheart.

325
00:10:36,474 --> 00:10:38,573
Who in your family made the dish

326
00:10:38,573 --> 00:10:40,804
everybody got when they were sick?

327
00:10:40,984 --> 00:10:42,498
I read every comment,

328
00:10:42,498 --> 00:10:45,310
and tomorrow I'll bring you another one

329
00:10:45,310 --> 00:10:46,464
from my kitchen.

330
00:10:46,644 --> 00:10:49,287
And if you want another one tonight,

331
00:10:49,287 --> 00:10:52,371
the whole playlist of Grandma's recipes is

332
00:10:52,371 --> 00:10:54,353
right there on your screen,

333
00:10:54,353 --> 00:10:56,409
and I'll see you in the next

334
00:10:56,409 --> 00:10:56,849
video.

335
00:10:57,029 --> 00:10:58,689
Good night, honey.
